6. Data model requirements
The logical domain model should include the following entities or equivalent structures:
Domain	Core records
Identity	User, credential, session, role assignment, consent
Taxonomy	Exam type, subject, topic, syllabus version
Content	Question, question version, source, review, content report
Assessment	Exam template, snapshot, attempt, answer mutation, submission receipt, score
Learning	Study plan, goal, streak event, topic performance
Classroom	Institution, membership, class, invitation, assignment
Commerce	Publisher, listing, order, payment, refund, entitlement, settlement
Rewards	Points ledger, commission ledger, referral, payout
Operations	Audit event, support case, moderation case, incident

Database integrity rules
- Use primary keys, foreign keys, unique constraints, and appropriate check constraints.
- Use database transactions for multi-record business operations.
- Use versioning or locking where concurrent writes can conflict.
- Preserve historical exam and scoring versions.
- Keep monetary calculations in fixed precision.
- Use explicit state machines for payments, submissions, entitlements, and payouts.
- Keep analytics rebuildable from source-of-truth records.
- Use an expand-migrate-contract strategy for database changes.
