3.3 Modular monolith rules
•	One deployable backend can contain independently owned modules with explicit package boundaries.
•	Modules must not write directly into another module’s tables except through an explicitly agreed shared transaction boundary. Prefer module services and documented ownership.
•	Each module owns its schema/tables, invariants, migrations and tests. Cross-module read models may be built intentionally.
•	Do not split into services until there is a demonstrated need: independent scaling, isolation, team ownership, regulatory boundary or deployment bottleneck.
•	Keep synchronous business-critical operations in one database transaction when consistency requires it; use an outbox/job pattern for reliable external side effects.
•	Do not introduce Kafka, Kubernetes, service mesh or a distributed cache layer solely for perceived sophistication.
3.4 Critical invariants
26.	The server owns assessment start time, deadline, attempt eligibility, submission state and score.
27.	An assessment session uses an immutable snapshot of its questions, options, scoring policy and relevant content versions.
28.	A client retry must not create duplicate responses, attempts, orders, ledger entries or payment effects.
29.	Correct answers and hidden explanations must not be exposed before policy permits them.
30.	A successful browser redirect is not proof of payment; verified provider evidence is required.
31.	Learning points and monetary commissions are different assets with different rules and ledgers.
32.	Every financial state transition is traceable to a business event and external provider reference where applicable.
33.	Every tenant-scoped read/write checks organization membership and permission server-side.
34.	Published content has provenance, licensing/permission metadata, review status and version history.
35.	A restore test must prove that backups are usable; a green backup status alone is insufficient.
3.5 Environments and release flow
•	Local: containerized dependencies or documented developer setup; seeded synthetic data only.
•	CI: formatting, linting, type checks, unit tests, security/dependency checks, migration validation, API contract checks.
•	Preview/staging: production-like configuration, isolated payment sandbox, test email provider, synthetic data, end-to-end smoke tests.
•	Production: least-privilege secrets, restricted admin access, backups, alerts, audit logs, release approvals and rollback procedure.
•	Use expand/contract database migrations for changes that overlap with rolling deploys; never rely on destructive migrations during the same release that removes old code paths.
•	Feature flags should protect risky rollouts, but must have an owner, expiry/review date and documented fallback.
