4.2 Relationships and cardinality
•	One user can hold multiple roles and belong to multiple organizations; a role is not a separate account.
•	An organization owns classes and organization-level assessments; class membership is distinct from referral attribution.
•	A question can have many immutable versions; assessment items point to a specific published question version.
•	An assessment definition can have many sessions; each session captures the exact definition/question/scoring snapshot used.
•	An order can have multiple items and payment attempts; one verified payment can create one or more entitlements according to the order policy.
•	A qualifying transaction may generate a commission ledger entry; points events are separate and may have different expiry/redemption rules.
•	Audit logs refer to resource identifiers and safe metadata; avoid copying sensitive student answers or payment credentials into logs.
4.3 Indexes and data integrity
•	Unique index on lowercased/normalized email; normalized phone if phone login is enabled.
•	Indexes on organization_memberships(user_id, status), classes(organization_id, status), class_memberships(class_id, status).
•	Questions: index on subject_id/topic_id/status; published question versions on review_state/published_at.
•	Sessions: index on user_id/started_at, assessment_definition_id/status, and deadline_at/status for expiry jobs.
•	Responses: unique(session_id, question_version_id); index on session_id.
•	Orders: index on user_id/created_at and status/created_at; provider events unique(provider,event_id).
•	Ledger entries: unique(idempotency_key), index(account_id, created_at), and source_type/source_id.
•	Use foreign keys and check constraints for nonnegative amounts, valid status fields and allowed time ranges; use transactions for multi-row state transitions.
•	Partition only after observed table growth and query evidence justify the operational complexity.
4.4 Data retention and privacy
•	Classify personal, student, assessment, payment-reference, support and analytics data; document purpose and access.
•	Collect only data needed for the product. Avoid unnecessary date of birth, precise location, sensitive profile fields or public student rankings.
•	Define retention/deletion/anonymization policies with legal/privacy review, especially for minors and institution-managed accounts.
•	Keep payment card data with the payment provider; store provider references and state, not raw card details.
•	Provide account export/deletion processes consistent with legal retention obligations and audit requirements.
•	Use synthetic or masked data in development and staging; do not copy production student records into test environments.
5. API contracts
All endpoints are under /v1. Use JSON, consistent error envelopes, pagination, request IDs, explicit authorization, schema validation and OpenAPI. Exact names can change during design, but contract changes require frontend/backend review.
5.1 API conventions
•	Authentication: secure HttpOnly cookies for same-site browser sessions where practical, or short-lived bearer access tokens with a documented refresh strategy. Protect state-changing cookie-authenticated endpoints against CSRF.
•	Authorization: server-side role + resource + tenant checks on every protected request.
•	Idempotency: accept Idempotency-Key for assessment start/submission, order creation, payment initiation and other retry-sensitive writes.
•	Concurrency: use resource version/ETag or version fields where concurrent updates matter; return 409 on stale/conflicting writes.
•	Pagination: cursor-based for large/rapidly changing lists; include next_cursor and has_more.
•	Errors: {"error":{"code":"VALIDATION_ERROR","message":"...","request_id":"...","details":[]}}. Do not expose stack traces or secrets.
•	Time: ISO-8601 UTC timestamps in API payloads; client localizes display only.
•	Money: {"amount_minor":150000,"currency":"NGN"}; define provider-specific minor-unit rules explicitly and never use floating point.
•	OpenAPI schema and generated client/types should be checked in CI to detect drift.
