  "started_at": "2026-10-26T10:00:00Z",
  "deadline_at": "2026-10-26T10:45:00Z",
  "server_time": "2026-10-26T10:00:00Z",
  "question_count": 40,
  "next_action": "answer"
}
The response should return only question data the learner is permitted to see. Do not send the answer key and rely on hiding it in the UI.
5.4 Response-save contract example
PUT /v1/assessment-sessions/{sessionId}/responses/{questionVersionId}
{
  "answer": { "selected_option_id": "option-b" },
  "client_sequence": 12,
  "expected_version": 3
}
 
Success:
{
  "saved": true,
  "server_saved_at": "2026-10-26T10:08:12Z",
  "version": 4,
  "session_status": "in_progress"
}
The backend validates session ownership, session status, deadline, question membership and answer schema. Duplicate requests must not create duplicate records. Define a deliberate policy for stale writes rather than silently overwriting newer responses.
5.5 Error codes
Code	HTTP	Meaning / client response
VALIDATION_ERROR	400	Input is invalid; show field-level feedback
UNAUTHENTICATED	401	Sign in or refresh session
FORBIDDEN	403	User lacks permission; do not reveal protected resource details
NOT_FOUND	404	Resource absent or intentionally hidden
CONFLICT	409	State/version/idempotency conflict; refresh state
ASSESSMENT_NOT_OPEN	409	Assessment not yet available or no longer available
SESSION_EXPIRED	409	Session deadline passed; fetch final status/result
RATE_LIMITED	429	Back off and retry according to Retry-After
PAYMENT_PENDING	202/409	Payment not yet verified; poll or wait for notification
INTERNAL_ERROR	500	Generic message and request ID; never leak stack traces

5.6 Webhook processing
36.	Receive the provider event over HTTPS and verify signature using the provider’s documented method.
37.	Validate event type, provider reference, amount and currency against the stored payment attempt.
38.	Persist a deduplicated provider event keyed by provider + event ID before applying effects.
39.	Process state transition transactionally and create entitlements/ledger events only once.
40.	Acknowledge valid events promptly; enqueue slow work when appropriate.
41.	Reconcile pending/ambiguous payments on a schedule; provide a manual review path.
42.	Never trust amount, currency, order status or entitlement claims supplied by the browser.
6. Assessment engine design
6.1 State machine
created -> in_progress -> submitted
                     \-> expired
                     \-> cancelled (only under defined policy)
State transitions are server-owned. A submitted or expired session cannot return to in_progress. A result may have a separate release state if teachers need to withhold feedback until an assessment window closes.
6.2 Required behaviors
•	Start endpoint checks availability window, membership, attempt limit and eligibility in a transaction.
•	Store started_at and deadline_at on the server. Client countdown is a display aid, not authority.
•	Persist a question snapshot or immutable version references before serving the first question.
•	Autosave on answer change and at a safe interval; use retry with exponential backoff and idempotent writes.
•	Expose clear save state: Saved, Saving, Offline / waiting to sync, or Save failed — retry required.
•	On reconnect, fetch authoritative session status before attempting writes; never assume a session remains open.
•	Submission and auto-expiry use the same finalization function so scoring behavior is consistent.
•	Scoring is deterministic and versioned. Record score policy and score breakdown used.
•	If randomized questions/options are used, persist the exact ordering for that attempt.
•	Support accommodations only through explicit policy and audited configuration, not client-side timer manipulation.
6.3 Offline policy
PWA offline support should initially prioritize shell/navigation and safe read-only study resources. For an active timed assessment, the product must clearly disclose whether answers are queued locally, how long they remain, and when server confirmation is received. Use IndexedDB only for a bounded, versioned queue; minimize sensitive content, define device-sharing behavior, and test storage quota/eviction. Never label an answer as saved until the server confirms it. Decide before launch whether high-stakes institutional exams require online-only mode.
6.4 Exam integrity and abuse
•	Do not claim browser-based proctoring is foolproof. Document the actual controls and limitations.
•	Limit answer-key exposure, enforce access windows, log suspicious patterns with privacy controls, and protect question bank endpoints.
•	Use rate limits and abuse monitoring for account creation, invitation redemption and repeated assessment attempts.
•	Define policies for reconnects, late joins, deadline races, duplicate tabs, device clock drift and teacher cancellations.
•	Maintain a content correction workflow. If a published question is defective, record affected sessions and a policy for score correction.
7. Payments, publications and rewards
7.1 Commerce principles
•	Order totals are calculated on the server from active price records and eligible discounts.
•	Keep order status, payment attempt status and entitlement status separate; they are related but not identical.
•	Use immutable order-item price snapshots so later price changes do not rewrite past purchases.
•	Create entitlements idempotently after verified payment or explicit free grant.
•	Protect publication files in private object storage; issue short-lived signed URLs after entitlement checks.
•	Implement refund/revocation policy and customer-support evidence before accepting real money.
•	Keep a daily payment reconciliation report comparing internal records with provider settlement/report data.
7.2 Suggested order states
draft -> pending_payment -> paid -> fulfilled
                      |       \-> partially_refunded -> refunded
                      \-> payment_failed / cancelled
The exact state model must match the selected payment provider and refund policy. Do not treat browser navigation to a success page as the paid transition.
7.3 Rewards policy
•	Maintain separate balances and ledgers for learning points and monetary commissions.
•	Version reward rules with effective dates. Each ledger entry records the exact rule version and qualifying source event.
•	Use pending, eligible, reversed and paid/settled states where a commission depends on a refund window or fraud review.
•	Do not reward recruitment alone as the economic event. Tie commissions to legitimate, qualifying product transactions or approved outcomes.
•	Set caps, eligibility rules, self-referral controls, duplicate-account detection, refund reversal rules and dispute windows.
•	Do not enable cash-out, transfer or stored-value functionality until qualified Nigerian legal/compliance advice and payment-provider review confirm the operating model.
•	Show users the rule, qualifying event, status, amount, timing and appeal path; do not imply guaranteed income.
7.4 Content rights and publisher operations
•	A question or book being available online does not automatically grant commercial reproduction rights.
•	Record source, author/publisher, license basis, permission evidence, attribution, reviewer and version.
•	Define takedown, correction, appeal, royalty/commission and content quality workflows before opening a multi-seller marketplace.
•	Separate content creator permissions from platform-admin permissions; require review before public listing.
8. UI/UX scope and design system
8.1 UX principles
•	Mobile-first and low-bandwidth aware; prioritize readable text, lightweight screens and predictable navigation.
•	The primary action on each screen must be obvious. Avoid dashboard cards that do not help a user make a decision.
•	Use progressive disclosure for complex teacher and institution configuration.
•	Treat empty, loading, error, offline, syncing, expired and permission-denied states as designed screens, not afterthoughts.
•	Accessibility target: WCAG 2.2 AA where feasible; keyboard navigation, visible focus, contrast, labels, semantic structure and screen-reader status announcements.
•	Do not expose all modules to every role in a generic dashboard; show role-relevant tasks and a role switcher where users have multiple roles.
•	Use plain-language payment, timer and reward status. Avoid misleading urgency or income claims.
