•	Release readiness review before every production rollout; post-release review after significant incidents.
•	Maintain architecture decision records (ADRs) for decisions with long-term consequences and a decision log for product trade-offs.
11.2 Ownership boundaries
•	Frontend owns presentation and local interaction state; backend owns authorization, assessment state, pricing, scoring, payment confirmation and ledger invariants.
•	Design owns interaction behavior and content hierarchy; product owner resolves trade-offs and accepts scope.
•	QA is involved during design and story definition, not only after implementation.
•	Operations owns procedures and exception queues; automated systems must not silently replace human review for high-risk financial/content actions.
•	A feature is not launch-ready until the operational team knows how to investigate failures and correct legitimate mistakes.
12. Testing strategy
12.1 Test pyramid
Layer	Coverage	Examples
Unit	Fast, deterministic domain logic	Scoring, timer policy, reward eligibility, order totals, permission policy
Database/integration	Real PostgreSQL and transaction behavior	Unique constraints, migrations, concurrent submissions, idempotency, ledger transactions
API contract	OpenAPI schema and frontend/backend compatibility	Request/response validation, error envelopes, pagination, auth requirements
Component	Interactive UI states	Question selection, timer display, validation, save-state banner, responsive navigation
End-to-end	Critical user journeys	Register → practice → result; teacher schedule → learner completes → teacher report; purchase → webhook → entitlement
Security	Abuse and authorization	IDOR, privilege escalation, CSRF/XSS, injection, rate limits, session invalidation, webhook forgery
Performance	Load and stress at realistic usage	Concurrent assessment starts/submissions, response autosaves, report generation, catalogue queries
Resilience	Failure/recovery behavior	Network drop, duplicate webhook, queue retry, DB failover, backup restore, deploy rollback
Accessibility/usability	Real interaction quality	Keyboard-only flows, screen reader status, contrast, mobile device testing, low bandwidth

12.2 Critical test scenarios
•	Two requests start the same attempt concurrently; only one valid attempt is created.
•	The client submits twice because the response was lost; score and result are finalized once.
•	A response write arrives at the deadline boundary; behavior is deterministic and documented.
•	The client clock is wrong or the user changes device time; server deadline remains authoritative.
•	A student refreshes, closes the tab or reconnects after a network outage; authoritative session state is recovered.
•	A user guesses another student’s session, class, order or entitlement ID; access is denied.
•	A teacher changes a draft assessment after a student starts; the existing session’s snapshot remains stable.
•	A payment webhook is duplicated, delayed, out of order or invalidly signed; no duplicate entitlement/ledger effect occurs.
•	A payment succeeds but the browser never returns; server-side processing still fulfills the order.
•	A refund arrives after a commission was provisionally calculated; a compensating ledger entry/reversal follows policy.
•	A published question is withdrawn or corrected; existing session history remains auditable and score-correction policy can be applied.
•	A database migration fails or deploy rolls back; data remains consistent and recovery procedure is tested.
•	An admin account is compromised or a staff member changes role; session revocation and audit review work.
12.3 Performance and reliability objectives
Define SLOs from product impact and pilot data rather than copying arbitrary industry targets. Before launch, choose explicit targets for API availability, assessment response-save success, submission success, p95 latency for key endpoints, recovery time objective (RTO), recovery point objective (RPO) and alert response. Set initial load-test assumptions for concurrent active learners, requests per active learner, question count, and peak exam windows.
•	Load test the critical assessment path at an agreed multiplier above expected pilot peak.
•	Measure p50/p95/p99 latency, error rate, database saturation, queue lag and response-save success.
•	Test bursts at scheduled exam start and end times; averages can hide deadline-related failures.
•	Test object-storage access, payment provider latency and background-job backlogs separately.
•	Run a restore exercise against a clean environment and verify data consistency, not just database startup.
13. Security, privacy and compliance workstream
13.1 Minimum controls
•	Threat model before production: account takeover, IDOR, privilege escalation, content theft, payment fraud, referral abuse, insider misuse and denial of service.
•	Secure password hashing or reputable identity provider; MFA for privileged accounts; short-lived sessions and revocation.
•	Rate limits for login, password reset, invitations, assessment creation, response writes and public endpoints.
•	Validate and normalize inputs; parameterized SQL; output encoding; secure headers; CSRF protection where cookie auth is used.
•	Encrypt traffic in transit and use provider-managed encryption at rest; tightly control secrets and rotate them.
•	Least privilege for database roles, cloud IAM, admin users and production access; periodically review grants.
•	Audit privileged operations and sensitive data access; avoid logging passwords, tokens, full payment payloads or unnecessary student responses.
•	Dependency and container scanning, secret scanning, static analysis and regular patching.
•	Document incident triage, evidence preservation, notification decisions and recovery responsibilities.
13.2 Nigeria-specific review items
Before real-money launch, obtain qualified advice on applicable Nigerian data-protection obligations, student/minor data and consent, consumer protection, tax/invoicing, content intellectual property, publisher contracts and the legal/payment-provider treatment of points, commissions, transfers or withdrawals. This blueprint is not legal advice. The product must not activate cash-out or stored-value-like features merely because a ledger has been implemented.
13.3 AI later, not a launch dependency
•	Ground responses in licensed, approved content; cite the relevant content version or source when possible.
•	Evaluate accuracy, unsupported claims, bias, prompt injection through retrieved content and privacy leakage.
•	Do not use student performance data for model training without a reviewed legal basis and transparent policy.
•	Provide an explicit fallback when retrieval confidence is low; route disputed educational answers to human review.
•	Track cost per successful learning outcome and latency; do not add AI simply to make the product appear modern.
14. Observability and operations
14.1 Required telemetry
•	Structured logs with request_id, trace_id, actor identifier where appropriate, route, status, latency and safe error code.
•	Metrics for request volume/errors/latency, DB pool usage, slow queries, background-job retries/lag, assessment save/submit success, payment state age, webhook failures and reconciliation differences.
•	Traces across API → database → queue/provider for high-value workflows.
•	Business dashboards for activation, assessment completion, content reports, purchases, refunds, rewards exceptions and support backlog.
•	Alerts must have an owner, severity, runbook and a clear action. Avoid alerts that merely report noise.
14.2 Runbooks required before launch
•	Assessment submission failure or widespread latency.
•	Payment provider outage, delayed webhooks and reconciliation mismatch.
•	Incorrect question/scoring incident and affected learner review.
•	Data exposure or suspected account compromise.
•	Database capacity/availability issue and backup restore.
•	Bad deployment, rollback and feature-flag disablement.
•	Content takedown, publisher dispute and referral/commission dispute.
•	Support escalation and communication to affected schools/learners.
